{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: fresh independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of stage S1, at exact head 643ab9c5f2ec30c2ce4c6240fd251236abc019c0 on request/lane-worktree-identity (pushed). This head answers your changes-requested verdict 19b37887 on 0d1c7e11, which I ratified in 6803776f.\n\nWhat governs it. Child request ac54fe71 (#22310), \"S1: deliver the governing design and read-only checkout association\", a stage child of #22168, itself governed by Hugh's effective adoption ba18cd1a of proposal 7a71886b (#22208) and the reconciled design. My promise on that child is a4c569b1, unchanged: the outcome, conditions, performer, destination and governing authority are the same, so the corrections stayed on the existing commitment rather than becoming a new one. The sibling stages are 0e7c04c8 (#22311, S2) and 8415ce89 (#22312, S3); neither is delivered or claimed here.\n\nWhat to read first. The reporting artifact is the internal/app/association.go artifact 94e9d744b46a294f85c1850554f2a9cdb87b32b6, published last among the artifacts resting on the promise. It states what your verdict asked for and what changed, then the conditions one by one, the proof-obligation to test table, the twenty omission controls and the gates. Sixteen companion artifacts stand at this same head, one per changed path; the seven whose path changed since 0d1c7e11 -- internal/app/association.go, internal/app/association_test.go, internal/gitstore/lineage.go, internal/gitstore/lineage_test.go, docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/reference/landing-observations.md and internal/app/app.go -- each cite the verdict and say what changed at that path.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE 0d1c7e11: six files, +476/-72, then two more files and +15/-8 of comments on top, described at the end of this section. Each of your findings, its fix, its regression and its omission control:\n\nFinding 1, cache validity. Fix: associationCacheKey gains a third input, associationCheckoutDigest over each captured checkout's label, branch, head and detached flag in the order given -- inputs only, never the Grade and Governing the pass writes back, which would key the cache on its own output. copyAssociationTable now also clones each claim's Candidates slice, not just the outer claims slice, so a cached table hands no caller a shared candidate list. Ref move and rename invalidation is retained. Regression: TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo, which builds two real unreferenced heads carrying the same trailer, captures one snapshot, and holds that the second detached checkout gets its own answer rather than the first one's cached row at unchanged refs and an unchanged frontier. Control: m18-cache-drops-checkouts, the digest replaced with a constant.\n\nFinding 2, exhaustion must report incomplete. Fix: associationTips returns an exhausted flag and detects the excess before dropping anything; Store.Lineage asks git for one commit past the per-tip limit, drops the extra and names the cut-off tip in LineageResult.Truncated; associate turns either into an incomplete table with a stated reason, empty rows and every checkout unknown. Regressions: TestMoreTipsThanTheLimitReadsIsIncompleteNotTruncated, which builds real local branches and holds that 256 answer and 257 report incomplete, counting the branches through git rather than through the bound under test; and TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated, the same one level down at 512 and 513 first-parent commits. You noted the per-tip case as source inspection rather than an executed counterexample; it is executed now. Control: m19-exhaustion-signal-dropped.\n\nFinding 3, missing objects and the annotation step. Fix: Lineage separates unheldTip (\"bad object\", \"bad revision\") from emptyHistory (an unborn repository, a complete answer of nothing) and names unheld tips in LineageResult.Unavailable; associate discards the batch on any unavailable tip; completion is decided in exactly one place, incompleteAssociation, with Associations calling unknownAssociationTable on an incomplete result and applyAssociationRows returning early rather than annotating from empty rows, so the unknown you identified can no longer be cleared to blank on the way out. Regression: TestAnUnavailableTipIsIncompleteAndNotAnEmptyHistory, and TestAssociationDiscardsTheWholeBatchOnBudgetExhaustion gained a cancelled-context leg at the public entry point. Control: m20-bad-object-is-success.\n\nFinding 4, your Simplification point about the report's own prose. The earlier report claimed cleanup reads \"the same table\" under \"the same cache\" while handleWorktrees calls ClassifyWorktrees and Associations separately. Corrected in the report and in both documentation pages: the association and the cleanup classification are two judgments over shared inputs -- one captured checkout listing, the same bounded ref inventory, the same 65,536-step budget and three-second deadline -- with separate caches keyed differently, neither reading the other's result, kept apart so an unsigned claim cannot reach a deletion decision.\n\nThen one comment correction, at 643ab9c5 over 1d7f3656: two files, +15/-8, every changed line inside a comment. The declaration-site comment on the Workspace association-cache fields had gone on describing a two-input key after the third input was added for finding 1, and the doc comment on TestAssociationCachesUnderTheFrontierAndRefDigest said the same thing. Both now name all three inputs and record that the grades this pass writes back are outputs that never key the cache. That test's name is deliberately unchanged: the checkout-digest half has its own named proof in TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo, and the m10 mutant evidence cites the existing name.\n\nYour three probes from your own review file were re-run unmodified against this tree in a throwaway file and all pass. The file is not committed; the three regressions above are named for the defects rather than for you.\n\nEvidence attached: gates-s1-v3.txt and mutants-s1.txt.\n\nGates, and exactly which stand where. Run fresh at this tree after the comment correction: gofmt -l ./internal ./cmd rc=0; go vet ./... rc=0; go build ./... rc=0; go test -count=1 ./internal/app/ rc=0; git diff --check rc=0 -- the \"comment fix\" section of the attached log. Carried from 1d7f3656, whose tree this head reproduces byte for byte outside those two comments: go test -count=1 over internal/app, internal/gitstore, internal/service, internal/reviewguard and internal/statusview rc=0; go test -race -count=1 ./internal/app/ rc=0, which was fresh at 1d7f3656 and is carried from there and from nowhere earlier; go test -count=1 ./... rc=0; and the twenty mutants, which were not re-run because a comment participates in no mutation. Both sections are in the one log, in the order they were run. One carried gate needs stating rather than summarising: the standalone go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... run failed once, rc=1, on TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord with \"refuse verified frontier rollback: depth 22449 is shorter than previously verified depth 22450\" -- the shared workroom log is appended to by other sessions while that gate reads it, and no file in this diff touches that path. The same package passed inside the go test ./... run that followed, and the recorded repeat of the standalone run is rc=0. Both the failure and the repeat are in the log.\n\nMutants, twenty, run against the 1d7f3656 tree and carried here. The seventeen earlier controls were re-run against that corrected tree and are all still killed; the patch scripts for m10, m11 and m13 were rewritten to target the corrected code, and the guard each removes is unchanged. m18, m19 and m20 are new, one per reproduced defect, and each is killed by its named regression. None survived.\n\nWhat I am asking you to check. Your architecture and security conclusions on 0d1c7e11 already covered the grading, the forged-trailer and copied-ident controls, exact-commit corroboration through the shared owned edge, foreign and unresolved handling, object re-verification, the absence of association-driven admission, and the cleanup protections. This round is the four corrections and whatever they disturbed:\n\n1. That the three-input cache key is complete -- that no other input the rows are derived from is missing from it -- and that only inputs, never outputs, take part.\n2. That every bounded stop now reports incomplete rather than a shorter answer: tip overflow, per-tip depth overflow, an unheld tip, budget exhaustion, cancellation, an unavailable ref inventory. And that a genuinely unborn or empty repository stays complete and distinguishable from a tip that has gone.\n3. That completion really is decided in one place, and that no path from Associations can annotate a view from an incomplete table.\n4. That the corrections turn no missing answer into negative evidence and weaken no existing cleanup protection, which is the constraint you set.\n5. That the architecture and landing-observations prose now matches the code on all three points -- cache inputs, incomplete conditions, and two judgments over shared inputs rather than one shared table.\n6. That the head still assigns and claims no S2 or S3 outcome, and that parent #22168 stays open.\n\nOne thing I am telling you rather than letting you find it: ui/src/lib/api.ts is still deliberately not extended and the wireparity gate does not cover WorktreeView, so no gate catches drift there. You did not make that a blocker last round and I have not changed it.\n\nMerge base. This head is one commit over main 359b211c. Current main is 1a5f1586, eighteen commits further on. git merge-tree --write-tree main 643ab9c5 is clean, producing tree c80c944c3f00e3a2565a0c761140c765f4bfce5c, so no rebase or recut is needed; the gap is ordinary staleness of the reasoning, not a changed head. The seventeen artifacts published at 0d1c7e11 are live at these same paths but sit outside the merge target on this branch, so they are not bases of this set and this merge does not retire them; I retire them separately naming these as successors. The in-target predecessors are unchanged from the previous head, re-derived from a projection at depth 22462.\n\nFile the verdict with gs review, naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first, so the lane resolves through the reporting artifact. I will ratify the report.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:94e9d744b46a294f85c1850554f2a9cdb87b32b6","conditions":"Fresh independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of the exact head 643ab9c5f2ec30c2ce4c6240fd251236abc019c0 on request/lane-worktree-identity, against child request ac54fe71 (#22310) under the same promise a4c569b1 and the adopted design ba18cd1a/7a71886b, reading the seventeen artifacts published at that head with the internal/app/association.go artifact as the reporting artifact. This head answers changes-requested verdict 19b37887 on 0d1c7e11, ratified 6803776f, in six files, +476/-72, followed by a comment-only correction of two files, +15/-8, that brings the association-cache field comment and one test doc comment into agreement with the three-input key. Confirm each finding is discharged: the association cache key names every input the rows are derived from and no output, including the captured checkout inventory, and cached tables are cloned to their candidate lists; every bounded stop -- tip overflow, per-tip depth overflow, an unheld tip, budget exhaustion, cancellation, an unavailable ref inventory -- reports incomplete with a reason, empty rows and every checkout unknown, while a genuinely empty or unborn repository stays complete and distinguishable from a tip that has gone; completion is decided in one place and no path can annotate a view from an incomplete table; and the architecture and landing-observations prose now describes the association and the cleanup classification as two judgments over shared inputs with separate caches, rather than one shared cached table the classifier does not consume. State the three conclusions explicitly. Architecture: name the affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md, say whether the head preserves or changes their contract, confirm the same-head paragraphs describe what the code does, and confirm the head neither delivers nor claims any outcome of stages 0e7c04c8 (#22311) or 8415ce89 (#22312) and does not close or narrow parent #22168. Security: confirm the corrections turn no missing answer into negative evidence, weaken no existing cleanup protection, and leave the trust boundary as reviewed -- an unsigned source trailer is still a claim, corroboration still requires a standing effective artifact plus the shared owned edge or adopted decision, no Git author ident is read, objects are still re-verified before any trailer read, and the association still reaches no admission or deletion decision. Simplification: identify any opportunity to simplify without weakening these conditions, including whether the single completion decision and the three-input key are expressed once rather than in parallel conventions. File the verdict with gs review naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first. Attached: gates-s1-v3.txt, which holds both sections in the order run -- gofmt, vet, build, go test ./internal/app/ and diff --check fresh at this head after the comment correction, and the wider set at 1d7f3656 including the race gate, whose tree this head reproduces byte for byte outside those two comments, with the standalone docset run's single live-log rollback failure and its rc=0 repeat both recorded; and mutants-s1.txt, twenty omission controls run against the 1d7f3656 tree and carried here, seventeen re-run and killed plus m18, m19 and m20, one per reproduced defect, not re-run at this head because a comment participates in no mutation.","head":"643ab9c5f2ec30c2ce4c6240fd251236abc019c0","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:19b37887a299a3e1a8dda2ec301e71e642e775a6, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:94e9d744b46a294f85c1850554f2a9cdb87b32b6, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ac54fe712abc7ca0614ca1067bad1a7ff8c490b0 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:005460183c819676fead7d19fdd20633b48dc16c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d and 189 more","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}